\node[card,anchor=north,text width=10cm,align=center,font=\small] at (1.5,-1.0)
  {Output recovers $\Rightarrow$ this component carries the information\\[2pt]
   {\footnotesize Path patching: patch only the path from one component into another}};
}
\end{tikzpicture}

\source{wang2022}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item The basic causal tool for circuits
\item Run the model on a clean prompt (answer: Mary) and a corrupted prompt that differs only in the relevant information (answer: John)
\item Copy one component's activation from the clean run into the corrupted run
\item If the output moves back towards Mary, that component carries the information that matters
\item Path patching refines this: only patch the effect of one component on another, to trace edges instead of nodes
\end{itemize}
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[shrink=4]{Path Patching}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth,height=0.75\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/wang2022/path_patching.png}
\end{center}

\source{wang2022}

\note{
\begin{itemize}
\item General method for finding a circuit: path patching
\item Specific task: indirect object identification (IOI)
\item IOI task: ``When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to'' $\to$ Mary. Metric: logit difference between the indirect object (Mary) and the subject (John)
\item We know what the relevant information is, so we construct a version with three unrelated random names (A, B, C) where the answer should be different
\item Activation patching patches a head's output and lets everything downstream recompute
\item Path patching patches only the path from one head into another, freezing all other heads to the original run: this tests edges, not just nodes
\item Can also knock out parts of the transformer, replacing a head's output with its mean over these random-name prompts
\item The authors traced the circuit for IOI through GPT-2
\end{itemize}
}
